---
import Shell from '../components/layout/Shell.astro';
import Formula from '../components/math/Formula.astro';
import StartQuiz from '../components/exercise/StartQuiz.svelte';
import LessonContent from '../components/content/LessonContent.astro';
import { notationLessons } from '../data/overview-lessons';
import { href } from '../data';
import FoundationVisual from '../components/visuals/FoundationVisual.svelte';
import { foundationTopics } from '../data/foundation-course';
---

<Shell
  title="Foundations"
  description="A short introduction to features, labels, training, evaluation, probability, and financial decisions."
>
  <p class="page-subtitle">
    Review the mathematics and vocabulary used in the algorithm lessons.
  </p>
  <div class="foundation-catalog">
    {foundationTopics.map((topic, index) => (
      <a class="foundation-card" href={href(`/foundations/${topic.slug}/`)}>
        <span class="eyebrow">Foundation {index + 1} · 4 lessons</span>
        <h2>{topic.title}</h2>
        <p>{topic.definition}</p>
        <span>Open lessons →</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
  <details class="optional-exploration">
    <summary>Loan example: inputs, probability, and expected loss</summary>
    <div class="foundations-reading">
      <section>
        <span class="eyebrow">1 · Frame the question</span>
        <h2>What can we know before approving a loan?</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="features" client:visible />
        <p>
          <strong>Features</strong> are the inputs available at decision time, such as a borrower’s
          debt-to-income ratio and recent missed payments. A <strong>label</strong> is the outcome
          observed later: whether a comparable past borrower defaulted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Information that only becomes available after the decision cannot be an input. Including
          it would make the model look better in a test than it can perform in use.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span class="eyebrow">2 · Learn, then predict</span>
        <h2>A model learns from examples</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="training" client:visible />
        <p>
          <strong>Training</strong> estimates a relationship between past features and labels.{' '}
          <strong>Prediction</strong> applies that learned relationship to a new applicant. During a
          prediction, the applicant’s eventual outcome is still unknown.
        </p>
        <p>
          A baseline gives you a useful starting comparison, such as assigning everyone the
          historical default rate. A more complex model needs evidence that it improves the
          decision.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span class="eyebrow">3 · Compare fairly</span>
        <h2>Keep an honest final test</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="splits" client:visible />
        <div class="foundation-splits">
          <div>
            <strong>Training</strong>
            <span>Learn model parameters.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Validation</strong>
            <span>Choose settings and thresholds.</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>Test</strong>
            <span>Evaluate the final choice once.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Repeatedly choosing settings using the test result turns the test into part of
          development. For time-dependent decisions, train on the past and evaluate on later
          observations.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span class="eyebrow">4 · Separate risk from action</span>
        <h2>A probability is an estimate; a threshold is a policy</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="probability" client:visible />
        <p>
          Suppose an applicant’s estimated default probability is 10%. A policy that reviews loans
          at or above 8% sends this applicant to review. A threshold of 12% does not. The estimated
          risk stays at 10% in both cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          A review threshold reflects error costs and available capacity. It does not transform a
          probability into certainty, and review does not automatically mean rejection.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <span class="eyebrow">5 · Connect probability to money</span>
        <h2>Expected loss is an average, not a guaranteed outcome</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="expected-loss" client:visible />
        <p>
          For a $10,000 loan with a 10% default probability and 50% loss if default occurs, expected
          loss is 0.10 × 0.50 × $10,000 = <strong>$500</strong>. An individual loan can produce a
          very different realized loss.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Read a formula in plain language</h2>
        <FoundationVisual id="formula" client:visible />
        <p>
          A symbol is a label. Read its definition, check the units, and then substitute values.
        </p>
        <Formula
          latex={String.raw`p=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}`}
          symbols={[
            { symbol: 'p', meaning: 'Estimated probability of default for this applicant.' },
            {
              symbol: 'z',
              meaning:
                'Weighted sum of this applicant’s features; a log-odds score. Zero maps to a 50% chance; a negative score maps below 50%.',
            },
            {
              symbol: 'e',
              meaning: 'The mathematical constant used by the exponential function in the sigmoid.',
            },
          ]}
          readAloud="The score z can be negative or positive, but a probability stays between 0 and 1. The sigmoid converts the score to that range. A score of zero gives 0.50, or a 50% estimated chance; a negative score gives less than 50%. This is an estimate, not a lending decision."
        />
        <StartQuiz client:visible />
        <details class="source-slide">
          <summary>More notation and symbol examples</summary>
          <FoundationVisual id="counts" notation client:visible />
          <LessonContent sections={notationLessons} level={3} />
        </details>
      </section>
    </div>
  </details>
  <div class="completion-row">
    <a class="completion-previous" href={href('/')}>
      ← Course outline
    </a>
    <a class="button" href={href('/algorithms/logistic-regression/')}>
      Start loan review →
    </a>
  </div>
</Shell>
